[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings where we're  bringing you part two of our cbs news  investigation into the california  medical board we sat down for an  exclusive interview with an insider who  says the system is costing patients  their lives yesterday you may recall we  introduced you to two patients who said  they were hurt by a doctor who has  already been under investigation by the  board and was nonetheless allowed to  continue practicing it's a perplexing  case our chief investigative  correspondent jim axelrod has been  digging into it perplexing and  concerning yeah good morning tony that  you know the stated mission of the  california medical board is to quote  protect health care consumers but our  cbs news investigative team found that  doctors who repeatedly hurt their  patients are often allowed to continue  practicing today we hear from two men  who say the system is broken and efforts  to reform it have failed for years  i'm speaking out because i can't sleep  at night  tj watkins was appointed to the  california medical board in 2019.  you  can't sleep at night because the way the  board does its business yes  watkins previously served as vice  president of the california board that  licenses physical therapists he's  speaking out now because he's troubled  by what he's seen on the medical board  the way they speak is always with doctor  care in mind  you never hear patient care ever  and i mean ever  do you think the way  the medical board functions in the state  of california  actually ends up costing patient lives  there's no question that it cost  patients lives watkins started tracking  discipline cases and says the california  medical board handed out more lenient  punishments than its guidelines  suggested in nine out of every 10 cases  so in ninety percent of the cases that  crossed my desk in the year 2020  we did not follow the guidelines cbs  news analyzed the last five years of  disciplinary decisions made by the  medical board which works hand in hand  with the attorney general's office  the board sanctioned more than 200  doctors for repeated acts of gross  negligence but allowed them to continue  practicing are you surprised at all  no  not at all because i'm in the room  i'm in the room trying to stop that from  happening  for the last year  watkins has been fighting to force the  board to hold doctors accountable the  process is not working for the public  but unfortunately it's working perfectly  perfectly for the doctors  one physician member on the board  chastised watkins last month for rocking  the boat in my eight years on this board  i have not encountered another board  member  who has been so  negative about our process as mr watkins  while the mission of the 15-member  california medical board is to quote  protect healthcare consumers  two of the 15 seats are occupied by  former presidents of the california  medical association which lobbies  lawmakers from its headquarters here in  sacramento on behalf of nearly 50 000  doctors in the state  did you ever run into an opponent as  fierce as the california medical  association no  never did  few know more about the california  medical association or the cma and its  lobbying power than jerry hill  their ability to  bring money to the table  to bring support  all factors into their ability to  control the medical board and control  legislation going forward  the former state senator chaired the  committee that oversees the board and  spent years of his political career  trying to make the medical board more  accountable today i introduced the  patient right to know act of 2018. the  cma  in your view  is it a powerful organization probably  one of the most powerful special  interests that we have here they are  masters at  getting what they want from any  legislation we analyzed the california  medical association's political  contributions and found that since 2015  they've spent nearly 15 million dollars  on lobbying and in donations to state  candidates  and during governor newsome's recall  election cma gave seven hundred fifty  thousand dollars to the california  democratic party  governor newsom's office declined to  comment the medical board said the law  requires it to rehabilitate doctors when  possible and told us that no matter what  the board follows its mission which is  to protect patients  as for the california medical  association they declined an interview  request but pointed out that it has  fought for public health during covet  and pushed for regulations that quote  protect patients from bad doctors  oh boy what's there at the end about the  california medical association but i  want to go back to mr watkins for a  second i'm in awe of him a current board  member who's speaking out against the  board do you think it's going to hurt  his standing on the board you know i  asked tj  what are you doing like you know what  what are you doing why all right what  are the consequences here and and he  grew up in south africa during apartheid  and it was very clear he said look and  he he his philosophy is  uh that you know in his words were evil  to triumph good men do nothing so to be  a good man yes in his view he had to  stand up and talk to us and that's what  that was all about for clarification  we're not talking about small botched  procedures like sometimes  these come to fatal endings for these  patients yes and then the investigations  in sue and  people these doctors continue to  practice absolutely  if i'm a patient this morning i'm not  feeling great in california knowing that  my board that's supposed to protect me  as lobbyists and doctors on it the right  millions of reasons why this system will  continue as you saw at the end yeah i  think that might change though thank you  mr watkins who said he couldn't sleep at  night thank you jim axelrod

